    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "When we look back, we realise just how much we’ve changed. It’s funny to think that the me from my childhood, who played with plastic soldiers, and the me today, with my taller and stronger body, are the same being. The people around me are different. My habits have changed. Even most of the cells in my body have been replaced. But here I am, still calling myself “me”. It’s strange, isn’t it?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Long before modern "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "psychologists",
            "zh": "心理学家"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " puzzled over this, the Greek historian Plutarch raised "
          },
          {
            "t": "underline",
            "v": "two similar questions"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". They became known as the “Ship of Theseus”."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Athens had to send a "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "tribute",
            "zh": "贡品"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " to King Minos of Crete. It consisted of seven young women and seven young men, who were to be eaten by the monster Minotaur. Theseus joined the third group to face the monster. He left on his ship, and after killing the Minotaur and going through various adventures, sailed back home. His ship was honoured in Athens for centuries. Over the years, it had to be repaired many times. The Athenians replaced the broken "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "planks",
            "zh": "木板"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " with new, stronger ones."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Plutarch asked: If every single part of the ship was replaced, was it still the same ship? If not, at what point did it stop being the same ship? Centuries later, "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "philosopher",
            "zh": "哲学家"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " Thomas Hobbes put forward another question: If all the old planks were collected after being replaced and used to build a second ship, which ship would be the real Ship of Theseus?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Some researchers argue that such puzzles arise because we expect the world to exactly match what’s in our heads. But science keeps reminding us that our brains can play tricks on us. Therefore, “The Ship of Theseus” is more about the limits of human thinking than the ship itself."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Seen this way, the ship is not a thing, not even a collection of existing parts. Rather, it’s more like an idea we carry in our minds. When Theseus thinks about his ship, he has expectations about what he can find in each place and how it carries him across the sea. As long as these connections remain, no matter which planks are used, it’s still his Ship of Theseus."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
